\ Native day-coordinate safety until arbitrary-precision dates exist.
\ CAL's ordinary number is signed 32-bit. The two day counters and
\ their sum must fit that type through every sauce computation.
\ Guard the public conversion before making any sauce or gate scan.
\
\ Foundation day is -15055671. Restrict both c and t to within
\ 400000000 days on either side of Foundation:
\   minimum -415055671, maximum 384944329.
\ Then every individual day count is at most 800000001, their sum
\ is at most 1600000002, and the inclusive distance at most 800000001.
\ These are all below the signed 32-bit limit 2147483647.
\ The extra 5778-day year window remains within native coordinates.
\
\ These bounds are a TEMPORARY safety guard, not the actual calendar
\ rules. Extending to arbitrarily distant days requires making all
\ absolute and relative day quantities arbitrary precision.
\ Fail closed rather than silently wrapping native numbers.

The native day limit pair are a thing with
  a calendar day called earliest accepted,
  a calendar day called latest accepted.

The native day limit pair are a native day limit pair.
The native date input validity is a flag.

To prepare native day limit pair:
  Put -415055671 into the native day limit pair's earliest accepted.
  Put 384944329 into the native day limit pair's latest accepted.

To inspect one native calendar day given a calendar day:
  If the calendar day is less than the native day limit pair's earliest accepted,
    put no into the native date input validity; exit.
  If the calendar day is greater than the native day limit pair's latest accepted,
    put no into the native date input validity.

To check native day coordinate inputs given a calendar day and another calendar day:
  Put yes into the native date input validity.
  Prepare native day limit pair.
  Inspect one native calendar day given the calendar day.
  If the native date input validity is no, exit.
  Inspect one native calendar day given the other calendar day.
